CODEX = {
    "af": ("Mira Cole", "Ashfall: Traces Kept Apart", "Mira Cole filed the village animals, the bodies from the ash lane and the mask found beside the clinic as separate observations. Fur at the well is not a record of who opened it. A familiar carving can be copied, inherited or placed there after the fact; it cannot stand in for a witness. She has kept the water readings, ash temperature and reed seed in different envelopes so the carriers can receive a safe route without accepting a story that the samples do not prove.\n\nRowan's notes let Mira reopen the shelter lane before the investigation is finished. The empty space beside each conclusion is deliberate. If another winter brings the same signs, the next clerk should know what was measured, what was moved and what the village only heard from the night watch."),
    "do": ("Nia Porter", "Dawnkeep: Drill Marks and Blank Steel", "Nia Porter compared the creatures in the practice yard with cadet formations. A reversed step can be learned by imitation, and a blank forge stamp is not a lost name. The bell answered from two places on the same watch, so she added a second caller instead of assigning the sound to a missing soldier. Each chalk line and wheel track remains in its own drawing.\n\nThe yard is usable again, but the record does not declare the machines part of the Order. Nia has kept the armourer's notes with the watch schedule so a later unit can repeat the checks. Safety can be restored on known paths while responsibility for the creatures remains an open question."),
    "db": ("Elara Wren", "Barracks Annex: Papers Without Senders", "Elara Wren catalogued paper seals, buttons and cuts by material and location. Several hands wrote on the seals, but none can yet be matched to the dispatch ledger. A blade line beyond a doorframe may have been made by an object that passed through the doorway, or by a damaged plate copied onto the wall. The annex is safe to cross; its unopened records remain sealed.\n\nThe creatures did not return the letters stacked in the barracks. Elara keeps the physical evidence beside the list of unanswered names without merging the two. A future archivist may find a match in another storehouse. Until then, the index records the empty sender field as carefully as the marks that can be seen."),
    "cw": ("Talia Mercer", "Cinderwell: Measures for a Dry Basin", "Talia Mercer's samples from the basin contain animal tracks, a green pit in brass and light seen through a closed jar. None changes the water gauge by itself. She put the mirror, salt crust, scale and wick on different pages so the crew can repeat each observation in the order it happened. The grain route is open again, which matters more to the workers than an early explanation.\n\nTalia has noted when a test failed to repeat. A dry shed can smell of rain, and water can hold a wake after a creature has moved on. She leaves the crop ledger free of monster names unless a harvest measurement depends on them. The next surveyor can compare the entries without inheriting a conclusion."),
    "cc": ("Talia Mercer", "Lower Cistern: The Gauge, the Bell and the Pool", "Talia Mercer logged the eel's arc, the shark's wake and the separated residue in three columns. The maintenance bell pulses with the copper veins of one golem, but the wheel does not. A number inside a claw resembles a tool mark only from one angle. She has preserved the ladder, gauge position and bell time so the cistern crew can repeat the readings after Rowan cleared the lower path.\n\nThe pool is no longer a safe place to reach by leaning over the sill. Talia added a rail and a paired-watch rule before the next sample is taken. Her diagrams leave blank space for measurements that contradict one another; a clean passage is a practical gain, not a settled account of how the creatures arrived."),
    "sr": ("Ruth Calder", "Salt Road: A Ridge of Unequal Evidence", "Ruth Calder recorded a turned reflection, cold hairs, static between nails and a buckle rusted on one face. The sightings happened along one road, but not at one time. She does not join them into a single track. The caravan can use the lower switchback while the smith compares the buckle with stored equipment and the watch repeats the static test after the next storm.\n\nThe road's salt crust changes under sun, wind and passing wheels. A print can look older when a fresh layer covers its edge. Ruth leaves the convoy list beside the map, not on top of it. The names show who received a crossing token; they cannot prove who saw each creature or who left a mark on the ridge."),
    "sl": ("Ruth Calder", "Lockhouse: A Seal Is Still a Boundary", "Ruth Calder measured dry prints around a wet shape, pressure bending near the lantern, cold points on a wall and a mark on slate. She kept the sealed drain shut throughout the survey. The lockhouse has taught its watch not to use a familiar voice as an identity, and a new silhouette deserves the same care. Her second observer saw the points fade before the sketch was complete; both pages remain in the folder.\n\nThe aisle is clear for clerks again. Ruth has not called the green growth a stain from the drain, because it crosses mortar untouched by water. A safe corridor and an unopened hatch can coexist. The next shift inherits both the key and the uncertainty."),
    "ch": ("Lyra Moss", "Choir: Notes That Cross the Water", "Lyra Moss charted seven lights, threads ending at a sounding post and an echo arriving before a ripple. The lamp narrowed once without losing wick, but the flame changed again after she moved the bell. She has kept both listener's timings and the failed second sketch. The choir can rehearse now that the gallery is clear; the singers have been asked not to turn a sound into a remembered voice.\n\nEach observation was made from a different mark on the floor. Water distorts distance, and stone carries a note around corners without carrying its source. Lyra leaves the old music sheets beside the new measurements but does not call the creatures the missing choir. Another listener may find the same intervals and still reach a different explanation."),
    "gw": ("The First Warden", "Greenwake: The Garden's Other Growth", "The First Warden allowed the gardeners to prune only the growth that blocked a working path. A dry ledger smelled of rain, a root followed the underside of a lintel and thorned plants opened toward the night crew. The untouched vine remains beside the trellis. No one has declared that it chooses a route; the garden may be responding to rain, light or a change in the posts.\n\nThe seed count is complete and the orchard can be tended. The Warden asked the next season's workers to keep one bed uncropped long enough to compare the roots. A living thing can be dangerous without being an omen, and a tended place does not belong to the Crown that records it."),
    "ow": ("Dr. Evelyn Hart", "Outer-Wake: Measurements Beyond a Baseline", "Dr. Evelyn Hart drew the web at its intersections with stone and metal, leaving the crystal shard in its socket. A magnetic needle moved once beside an iron form; mismatched tracks shared a stride, and pale caps appeared to move while the soil stayed level. These readings have no reliable local baseline. She keeps the earlier plate beside the field map with a scale marker visible on both.\n\nThe survey stakes did not shift. Evelyn will repeat the reading before calling the caps mobile or the socket part of the archive mechanism. The Outer-Wake creatures are present and dangerous, but their presence alone cannot prove which side of the Veil they remember. A later comparison may identify the same patterns in Asterra."),
    "lo": ("Captain Alden Vale", "Crownfall: Weather at the Oath Seal", "Captain Alden Vale wrote the fire and frost traces beside, not over, the old oath seal. One line cooled a chamber; another stopped at the seal's edge. Sparks answered to a name spoken aloud, while smoke followed a ceiling crack. Alden preserved the two lamp positions and covered the names before leaving the chamber. Rowan cleared a path for witnesses without turning the marks into another roll call.\n\nThe final record does not describe the creatures as proof for either ending. If the cycle continues, someone may read the marks as a warning to obey the Crown. If the Veil is crossed, another reader may treat the same page as evidence that the world can survive without the old arithmetic. Alden leaves that argument to whoever inherits the ledger."),
}
